--TEST--
`Core\Cli\Text::styled` neutralizes its body exactly as `Core\Cli\Text::plain` does, over the whole control range and counted — the constructor that emits control bytes on purpose is the one a program's own bytes could hide in — `rule:tooling/terminal-output-is-a-sink` and `rule:tooling/styling-is-a-value-not-a-grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 2: both constructors *"apply § 1's substitution to their input"*. `plain`
// is the one a case reaches for, and `styled` is the one that matters: it is
// the only thing in the language that puts an `ESC` in front of a program's
// text, so an escape it failed to substitute in the body would arrive in the
// company of ones it emitted itself. The invariant is that the control bytes
// in a `Cli\Text` all came from the `Cli\Style` — and this asks it of every
// C0, of `DEL` and of every C1 rather than of one hand-picked `\e[31m`.
class Styled {
    // § 1's table: the U+2400 block for a C0, U+2421 for `DEL`, and U+FFFD for
    // a C1, which has no glyph of its own.
    public static function glyph(uint $point): string {
        if ($point < 32) {
            return Core\Str::fromCodePoint((9216 as uint) + $point);
        }
        if ($point == 127) {
            return Core\Str::fromCodePoint(9249 as uint);
        }
        return Core\Str::fromCodePoint(65533 as uint);
    }

    // Read back with `as string` rather than echoed: `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` renders a
    // carrier as the bytes it already holds, so this is what the constructor
    // stored and not what a sink made of it.
    public static function counts(Core\Cli\Style $style, bool $bare): string {
        int $rows = 0;
        int $idempotent = 0;
        int $plain = 0;
        int $commands = 0;
        int $shown = 0;
        uint $point = 0;
        while ($point < 160) {
            if ($point < 32 || $point > 126) {
                string $byte = Core\Str::fromCodePoint($point);
                string $raw = "a" . $byte . "b";
                $rows = $rows + 1;

                // Idempotence through the styled path, which holds at any
                // colour depth: the opening sequence is the style's and is the
                // same both times, so a body that escaped twice — the second
                // escape `rule:security/capture-answers-the-carrier` exists to prevent — shows up as a
                // difference here whether or not the run has a terminal.
                string $once = Core\Cli\Text::styled($raw, $style) as string;
                string $again = Core\Cli\Text::styled(Core\Cli::escape($raw), $style) as string;
                if ($once == $again) {
                    $idempotent = $idempotent + 1;
                }

                if ($bare) {
                    // § 3 left this stream at `ColorDepth::None`, so the style
                    // contributes nothing and the two constructors must answer
                    // the same bytes for the same input — the whole difference
                    // between them is the styling that was dropped.
                    string $flat = Core\Cli\Text::plain($raw) as string;
                    if ($once == $flat) {
                        $plain = $plain + 1;
                    }
                    if ($point != 9 && $point != 10) {
                        $commands = $commands + 1;
                        // Both halves, because § 1 is a substitution: the byte
                        // is gone and its own glyph is there.
                        if (!Core\Str::contains($once, $byte)
                            && Core\Str::contains($once, Styled::glyph($point))) {
                            $shown = $shown + 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            $point = $point + (1 as uint);
        }

        string $lines = "styling is idempotent over the control range "
            . $idempotent as string . " of " . $rows as string;
        if ($bare) {
            return $lines . "\n"
                . "a styled body is a plain body " . $plain as string . " of " . $rows as string . "\n"
                . "a styled body shows every command glyph " . $shown as string . " of " . $commands as string;
        }
        return $lines . "\n"
            . "held (a terminal would style these)" . "\n"
            . "held (a terminal would style these)";
    }
}

// Four axes and both colour slots, so the opening sequence is a long one: a
// body byte that survived would have plenty of company to hide in.
var $style = Core\Cli\Style::of({
    color: Core\Cli\Color::RED,
    background: Core\Cli\Color::index(17),
    bold: true,
    underline: true
});
bool $bare = Core\Cli::colorDepth() == Core\Cli\ColorDepth::None;
echo Styled::counts($style, $bare), "\n";

// The empty style is the same constructor with nothing to emit, and it is
// still `styled`: the neutralization is not something the styling does on the
// way past.
echo Styled::counts(Core\Cli\Style::of({}), $bare), "\n";
--EXPECT--
styling is idempotent over the control range 65 of 65
a styled body is a plain body 65 of 65
a styled body shows every command glyph 63 of 63
styling is idempotent over the control range 65 of 65
a styled body is a plain body 65 of 65
a styled body shows every command glyph 63 of 63
